  "worked_solution": "First, to simplify the fraction on the left-hand side, we multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}-\\text{i}} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{5(\\sqrt{2}+\\text{i})}{(\\sqrt{2}-\\text{i})(\\sqrt{2}+\\text{i})} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{5(\\sqrt{2}+\\text{i})}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}-(\\text{i})^{2}} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{5(\\sqrt{2}+\\text{i})}{2+1} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{5(\\sqrt{2}+\\text{i})}{3} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}\\text{i} & =p+\\text{i}q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get $\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{3}=p.$\n\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get $\\frac{5}{3}=q.$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ and $q=\\frac{5}{3}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
